If it is shown that the land is actually cultivated either presently or in the immediate past or if it is shown that it is lying fallow, but is capable of being cultivated,

so long as the land has not been actually diverted to purposes other than agricultural purposes by construction of buildings thereon and other operations which render the land itself incapable of being cultivated without undertaking some other operations for making it fit for carrying on agricultural operations, it can be said to answer the description, of agricultural land in its widest significance,"
Again at page 355 summarising the position it is said:
"(1) The words "agricultural land" occurring in
Section 2 (3) (i)
of the Wealth Tax Act should be given the same meaning as the said expression bears in Entry 86 of List I and given the widest meaning;
(2) The said expression not having been denned in the Constitution, it must be given the meaning which it ordinarily bears in the English language and as understood in ordinary parlance;
(3) The actual user of the land for agriculture is one of the indicia for determining the character of the land, as agricultural land;
